2026-10-07T14:37:32.0079434Z [36;1m# local fork of each upstream lint aspect (clang-tidy, ruff, ...)[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0079683Z [36;1m# that skips such targets at analysis time. An earlier iteration of[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0079916Z [36;1m# this workflow did exactly that (see git history for[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0080142Z [36;1m# tools/lint/clang_tidy_lint_gate.bzl), but duplicating aspect[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0080401Z [36;1m# implementation logic per-linter is brittle: it silently drifts out[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0080666Z [36;1m# of sync with upstream `aspect_rules_lint` (new rule kinds, changed[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0080911Z [36;1m# `filter_srcs`/`should_visit` semantics, etc. would need to be[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0081268Z [36;1m# mirrored by hand in every fork), and any *unintended* coverage gap[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0081522Z [36;1m# it introduces (e.g. a target that should be linted but isn't) is[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0081781Z [36;1m# invisible here — it would only ever surface indirectly, if at all,[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0082032Z [36;1m# via our coverage metrics/dashboards flagging unexpectedly low[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0082274Z [36;1m# linter coverage for a package. Filtering empty reports in CI[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0082523Z [36;1m# keeps all targets flowing through the exact same upstream aspects[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0082774Z [36;1m# everyone else uses, at the cost of only being able to detect gaps[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0083026Z [36;1m# via those coverage metrics rather than failing the build outright.[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0083227Z [36;1mREPORTS=""[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0083356Z [36;1mfor f in ${ALL_REPORTS}; do[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0083504Z [36;1m  if [[ -s "${f}" ]]; then[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0083649Z [36;1m    REPORTS+="${f}"$'\n'[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0083782Z [36;1m  fi[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0083890Z [36;1mdone[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0084030Z [36;1mREPORTS=$(echo "${REPORTS}" | sed '/^$/d')[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0084187Z [36;1m[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0084300Z [36;1mif [[ -z "${REPORTS}" ]]; then[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0084515Z [36;1m  echo "::error::No non-empty SARIF reports found; nothing to merge/upload."[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0084728Z [36;1m  exit 1[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0084838Z [36;1mfi[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0084942Z [36;1m[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0085101Z [36;1m# Stage every per-file SARIF report as a symlink, numbered to avoid[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0085349Z [36;1m# basename collisions, in a single flat directory. Handing[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0085594Z [36;1m# `Sarif.Multitool merge` one glob specifier over that directory[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0085837Z [36;1m# (rather than hundreds of individual file paths) keeps the[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0086080Z [36;1m# invocation to a single argv entry no matter how many report files[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0086470Z [36;1m# there are, avoiding shell/xargs argument-length limits that can[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0086730Z [36;1m# otherwise silently corrupt the output (as previously happened with[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0086983Z [36;1m# `xargs jq -s ...`, which split into multiple invocations once the[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0087230Z [36;1m# report list exceeded xargs' default ~128KiB argument limit, each[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0087480Z [36;1m# appending a separate SARIF document to the same output file).[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0087713Z [36;1m# `Sarif.Multitool` expands this glob itself (via .NET's[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0087956Z [36;1m# Directory.EnumerateFiles filter matching), so it must stay quoted[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0088177Z [36;1m# here to survive as one argv entry.[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0088325Z [36;1mi=0[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0088442Z [36;1mwhile IFS= read -r f; do[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0088582Z [36;1m  i=$((i + 1))[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0088769Z [36;1m  ln -s "$(readlink -f "${f}")" "${STAGE_DIR}/report_$(printf '%06d' "${i}")"[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0089082Z [36;1mdone <<< "${REPORTS}"[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0089211Z [36;1m[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0089371Z [36;1m# Merge all per-file SARIF reports into a single SARIF document via[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0089626Z [36;1m# the official Microsoft SARIF SDK CLI. `sarif merge` coalesces runs[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0089879Z [36;1m# by tool name/version into one run per distinct tool by default[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0090131Z [36;1m# (deduplicating identical results), which is required since GitHub[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0090386Z [36;1m# Code Scanning (as of 2025-07-22) rejects SARIF uploads containing[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0090618Z [36;1m# multiple runs that share the same tool/category.[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0090789Z [36;1m#[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0091038Z [36;1m# `--merge-empty-logs` is required: by default, `merge` silently[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0091287Z [36;1m# *drops* any run whose `results` array is empty (i.e. a genuinely[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0091535Z [36;1m# clean lint pass for that file) instead of including it with zero[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0091782Z [36;1m# results. When every input report happens to be clean, that[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0092023Z [36;1m# default produces a `{"runs": []}` document with *no* runs at all,[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0092265Z [36;1m# which GitHub's SARIF upload API then rejects outright with[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0092513Z [36;1m# "Invalid request. 1 item required; only 0 were supplied." rather[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0092731Z [36;1m# than accepting it as "zero findings".[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0092923Z [36;1mnpx --yes "@microsoft/sarif-multitool@5.6.0" \[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0093100Z [36;1m  merge "${STAGE_DIR}/*" \[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0093248Z [36;1m  --merge-empty-logs \[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0093421Z [36;1m  --output-directory "$(dirname "ruff.sarif")" \[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0093616Z [36;1m  --output-file "$(basename "ruff.sarif")"[0m
2026-10-07T14:37:32.0093774Z [36;1m[0m
